 international athletics competitions in Paris in 1922 and in Göteborg in 1926. Spurred on by this competing organization, the " [:a {:href "/organizations/6"} "IAAF"] " relented and allowed women to compete in Amsterdam in five events – the " [:a {:href "/results/57875"} "100 metres"] ", " [:a {:href "/results/57891"} "800 metres"] ", " [:a {:href "/results/57897"} "4x100 relay"] ", " [:a {:href "/results/57902"} "high jump"] ", and " [:a {:href "/results/57914"} "discus throw"] ". In the 800 metres, the event was won by German " [:a {:href "/athletes/69945"} "Lina Radke-Batschauer"] ", but several of the women in the event collapsed in exhaustion after the race (not unlike the men, actually). But the " [:a {:href "/organizations/1"} "IOC"] " and IAAF considered this dangerous to women, and women would not run more than 200 metres at the Olympics again until " [:a {:href "/results/59982"} "1960"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "No British women competed in 1928, as the Women’s Amateur Athletic Association elected to save themselves for the 1930 Women’s World Games, in Praha, Czechoslovakia, where there were more events (11) for them to compete in, as opposed to only five in Amsterdam, their " [:em {} "raison d’etre"] " being that the Praha meeting better answered the purpose for World’s championships."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the sprints, Canada’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/66279"} "Percy Williams"] " became the first non-American to win the 100/200 double. Finland continued to star in the distance races, winning all the events longer than 800 metres, with " [:a {:href "/athletes/67728"} "Paavo Nurmi"] " back winning gold in the " [:a {:href "/results/57705"} "10,000 metres"] ", and silvers in the " [:a {:href "/results/57699"} "5,000 metres"] " and in the " [:a {:href "/results/57735"} "steeplechase"] "."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Events"] "\n" [:table {:class "table table-striped"} "\n" [:thead {} "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Event"] "\n" [:th {} "Status"] "\n" [:th {} "Date"] "\n" [:t